// EXPECT: FAIL
// ERROR_CONTAINS: requires@
// A CLOSED precondition — no free variable after substituting a constant argument — is decided
// absolutely by the solver, independent of any branch path condition. So a definitely-violating
// constant call inside an `if` (`g(0 - 5)` against `requires(x > 0)`) is discharged and REJECTED at
// ANY depth, with no over-rejection risk. Before the closed-arg-discharge slice this was fail-open
// (the statement-position discharge was gated to unconditional depth, so it slipped through in a branch
// and trapped only at runtime). A VARIABLE-arg branch call whose provability depends on the guard stays
// deferred — only closed preconditions discharge in a branch.
fn g(x: i64) requires(x > 0) { assert(x > 0); }
fn caller(b: bool) { if b { g(0 - 5); } }
fn main() { caller(true); }
